    <!-- COMPUTE COST -->
    <section>
      <p class="pt-tag pt-tag-val mb-3">Compute cost</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-4">PTC is analytic — no SCF, no iteration</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 max-w-4xl">
        At comparable accuracy, PTC is several orders of magnitude faster
        than DFT. Measurements made on the same subset of
        <strong>{m.timing.ptc.n} molecules</strong> (Bin A+B+C, n ≤ 12)
        and the same machine ({m.timing.machine_note}). Single-thread
        for both methods. PTC derives its observables analytically from
        s = 1/2, with no SCF cycle, no Hartree-Fock diagonalisation, no
        exchange-correlation integrals to evaluate.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="text-sm font-sans w-full">
          <thead class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-stone-300 text-xs text-stone-600 uppercase">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left py-2 px-3">Scope</th>
              <th class="text-right py-2 px-3 tabular-nums">N mol</th>
              <th class="text-right py-2 px-3 tabular-nums text-violet-800">B3LYP / def2-TZVP</th>
              <th class="text-right py-2 px-3 tabular-nums text-thmblue">PTC</th>
              <th class="text-right py-2 px-3 tabular-nums">Speedup</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-slate-700">
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100">
              <td class="py-2 px-3">A+B (n ≤ 8)</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right tabular-nums">{m.timing.b3lyp_def2tzvp.per_bin['A+B (n≤8)'].n}</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right tabular-nums">{Math.round(m.timing.b3lyp_def2tzvp.per_bin['A+B (n≤8)'].total_s / 60)} min {Math.round(m.timing.b3lyp_def2tzvp.per_bin['A+B (n≤8)'].total_s % 60)} s</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right tabular-nums font-bold text-thmblue">{m.timing.ptc.per_bin['A+B (n≤8)'].total_s.toFixed(2)} s</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right tabular-nums font-bold">× {Math.round(m.timing.b3lyp_def2tzvp.per_bin['A+B (n≤8)'].total_s / m.timing.ptc.per_bin['A+B (n≤8)'].total_s).toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100">
              <td class="py-2 px-3">C (n = 9-12)</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right tabular-nums">{m.timing.b3lyp_def2tzvp.per_bin['C (n=9-12)'].n}</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right tabular-nums">{Math.floor(m.timing.b3lyp_def2tzvp.per_bin['C (n=9-12)'].total_s / 3600)} h {Math.round(m.timing.b3lyp_def2tzvp.per_bin['C (n=9-12)'].total_s % 3600 / 60)} min</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right tabular-nums font-bold text-thmblue">{m.timing.ptc.per_bin['C (n=9-12)'].total_s.toFixed(2)} s</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right tabular-nums font-bold">× {Math.round(m.timing.b3lyp_def2tzvp.per_bin['C (n=9-12)'].total_s / m.timing.ptc.per_bin['C (n=9-12)'].total_s).toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="font-semibold bg-slate-50 border-b border-stone-200">
              <td class="py-2 px-3">Total (n ≤ 12)</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right tabular-nums">{m.timing.ptc.n}</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right tabular-nums">{Math.floor(m.timing.b3lyp_def2tzvp.total_s / 3600)} h {Math.round(m.timing.b3lyp_def2tzvp.total_s % 3600 / 60)} min</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right tabular-nums font-bold text-thmblue">{m.timing.ptc.total_s.toFixed(2)} s</td>
              <td class="py-2 px-3 text-right tabular-nums font-bold">× {Math.round(m.timing.b3lyp_def2tzvp.total_s / m.timing.ptc.total_s).toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-lg border-2 border-emerald-300 bg-emerald-50 p-4 mb-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-emerald-900 leading-relaxed">
          <strong>At a glance:</strong> what B3LYP/def2-TZVP does in
          <strong>1 h 56 min</strong> on 536 molecules, PTC does in
          <strong>{m.timing.ptc.total_s.toFixed(1)} seconds</strong>
          on the same machine. PTC median: <strong>{m.timing.ptc.median_ms} ms per molecule</strong>.
          The speedup grows with size — × 2,130 on small molecules,
          × 16,000 on 9-12 atom molecules — because DFT complexity
          scales as O(N³ to N⁴) while PTC remains nearly linear.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="rounded border border-slate-200 bg-white p-3 mb-6">
        <img src="/benchmark/charts/timing_log.svg"
             alt="Compute cost PTC vs B3LYP/def2-TZVP, log scale"
             class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="text-xs text-stone-600 mt-2">
          Total time per scope, log scale (the ratio is too large for a
          linear axis). Speedup factors are annotated in red above each
          pair of bars.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4">
        <h4 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-2">Why such a gap?</h4>
        <ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
          <li><strong>B3LYP/def2-TZVP</strong> requires for each
          molecule: basis set construction (~30 functions per heavy atom
          with def2-TZVP), SCF iteration to convergence (~15-30 cycles),
          exchange-correlation integrals on a radial grid, Hessian
          computation for harmonic ZPE. Cost scales as O(N³) to O(N⁴).</li>
          <li><strong>PTC</strong> directly evaluates a closed-form
          analytic expression per bond (Shannon cap × modular screening),
          and assembles D_at by graph summation on the Z/3 × Z/5 × Z/7
          torus. No SCF, no iteration, no grid. Near-linear scaling.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CAVEATS -->
    <section>
      <p class="pt-tag pt-tag-id mb-3">Honesty</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-4">Caveats and methodology</h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
          <h4 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-2">No comparison to G4 / W1 / CCSD(T)</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            Composite methods reach ~0.04 eV accuracy — but with
            ~10 fitted parameters calibrated on G2/G3. B3LYP is the
            most-used DFT standard in production chemistry; that was the
            relevant target.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
          <h4 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-2">Geometries</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            RDKit MMFF (3+ atoms) or fixed diatomic table for diatomics.
            Identical geometries between 6-31G* and def2-TZVP for an
            apples-to-apples comparison.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
          <h4 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-2">Zero-point energy (ZPE)</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            Harmonic ZPE B3LYP/6-31G* × 0.9806 (Scott & Radom 1996).
            For def2-TZVP, ZPE recycled from 6-31G* (less than 2%
            difference between bases, negligible relative to D₀ error).
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
          <h4 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-2">B3LYP failures excluded</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            9 SCF/Hessian failures (NO₂, ClF₃, B₂H₆, fullerene…) and
            8 pathological |err| &gt; 5 eV outliers (S₂F₁₀ −114 eV,
            PCl₅, SF₆, SF₄, HBO₂, phenanthrene, SO₃) excluded on the
            B3LYP side for fair comparison. <strong>PTC computes them
            without issue</strong>, and their values are included in
            benchmark 1 (1000 mol).
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
          <h4 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-2">Known PTC regressions</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            Aromatic N-heterocycles (pyrrole, indazole, pyridazine,
            triazine), siloxanes: PTC systematically over- or
            under-estimates. Open work items, scheduled for the next
            versions.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
          <h4 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-2">Heavy elements</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            Complexes with Ag, Au, heavy transition metals or I are
            computed by PTC but not by B3LYP/6-31G* (124 mol affected).
            Present in benchmark 1, excluded from benchmarks 2 and 3.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

